derivations.claim:
*Flow on real points.* Fix $
u\in O_h$. Regarded as a real vector field on the open subset $D_{r,s-4\sigma,h}$ of $\mathbb C^{2n}\cong\mathbb R^{4n}$, the field $X_F$ is $C^1$ and, by step 1.1 and [F1], $|X_F|\le|F_\vartheta|+|F_I|\le 2CE\,\sigma^{n+1}\cdot(r+\sigma)\le 4CE\sigma,\qquad |DX_F|\le CE$ on $D_{r/2,s-4\sigma}$ (each entry of the Jacobian of $X_F=(-F_\vartheta,F_I)$ is one of the displayed second derivatives, up to constants). Choose $c$ small enough that $CE\le1/2$ and $4CE\sigma\le\sigma/2$; then [F4] with $M_0=4CE\sigma$, $L_0=CE$, $R_0=\sigma/2$ shows that for every point of $B_{r/2}	imes S_{s-8\sigma}$ the time-one map exists and stays in $B_{3r/4}	imes S_{s-7\sigma}$, moving the point by at most $4CE\sigma$ in the unweighted norm and having $|D\Phi_1-\mathrm{id}|\le2CE$; more precisely, the estimates of [F4] applied to the components $I$ and $\vartheta$ with the weighted norm $W$ give $|W(\Phi_1-\mathrm{id})|\le CE,\qquad |W(D\Phi_1-\mathrm{id})W^{-1}|\le CE,$ because $|F_\vartheta|\le CEr\cdot\sigma^{n+1}$ and $|F_I|\le CE\sigma\cdot\sigma^{n+1}$ control the two weighted components and the Jacobian entries are bounded by $CE$ (step 1.1). For the parameter derivative, differentiate the ODE $z'=X_F(z,
u)$ with respect to $
u_j$: the derivative $\partial_{
u_j}\Phi_t$ satisfies the linearized equation with inhomogeneity $\partial_{
u_j}X_F$ and zero initial datum, and $|\partial_{
u_j}X_F|\le(2/h)|X_F|$ by [F2]'s Cauchy bound in $
u$ with margin $h/2$; Gronwall ([F4]) gives $|\partial_{
u_j}\Phi_1|\le2CE\sigma\cdot 2/h\cdot e^{CE}$, i.e. $|W(\partial_{
u_j}\Phi_1)|\le CE/h$ after absorbing constants; equivalently $h|W\partial_{
u_j}\Phi|\le CE$ on the smaller parameter domain $O_{h/2}$. This is clause 2's last estimate. [step 1.1, F1, F2, F4, algebra]
